-- =====================================================================
-- Level 2 Writing prompt bank.
--
-- 200 customer-email prompts and 200 non-email prompts, every one a distinct scenario.
-- WritingService serves one of each per attempt, so 100 users taking 2 attempts each over a
-- year draw 200 from either pool -- meaning no two candidates need ever share a task.
-- ContentAssignmentService additionally excludes any prompt a candidate has already been
-- served, so a retake never repeats their own.
--
-- Seeded behind a seed_state marker rather than a row-existence guard. data.sql's Level 2
-- insert checks NOT EXISTS (... WHERE level = 2), which is already true in production, so rows
-- appended there would never load on a live database. Bump the key to -v2 to push a revision.
--
-- Every prompt keeps the Level 2 shape agreed for this bank: a short scenario a fresher can
-- read once, then four plain instructions. The step up from Level 1 is that the situation is
-- real migration work with several things to hold together, not that the wording is denser.
--
-- Avoid apostrophes: these are single-quoted SQL literals.
-- =====================================================================

-- ---------- Customer email prompts, part 1 of 5 ----------
INSERT INTO writing_prompt (created_at, updated_at, category, prompt, level, difficulty)
SELECT now(), now(), 'Customer Email', v.prompt, 2, 'MEDIUM'
FROM (VALUES
    ('A customer reports that thirty shared links stopped working after their move from Google Drive to SharePoint. The links were rebuilt with new addresses, and a mapping file is ready to send. Write a reply. Explain why the old links changed, describe the mapping file you are attaching, give a date by which they should update their intranet, and offer a call if the list is long.'),
    ('A customer asks why their Box migration is only forty percent complete after two days when you quoted three days in total. The remaining content is smaller files that move faster, so the finish date has not moved. Write a reply. Explain why progress is not a straight line, confirm the original finish date, say what you will send them next, and thank them for checking.'),
    ('A customer wants to add ninety more users to a migration that is already scheduled. Adding them is possible but pushes the cutover back by two days. Write a reply. Confirm you can include them, state the new cutover date plainly, explain the two-day cost in one sentence, and ask for written approval before you replan.'),
    ('A customer emails at nine in the evening saying a director cannot find last years contracts after the cutover. The folder was moved under an archive parent, not deleted. Write a reply. Tell them the files are safe, give the exact new location, say what you will check tonight, and give a time tomorrow when you will confirm.'),
    ('A customer has been sent an invoice that includes eight thousand dollars of weekend engineering they did not expect. The weekend work was approved by their project manager in writing. Write a reply. State the amount calmly, point to the approval, offer to forward that email, and give a contact for billing questions.'),
    ('A customer asks whether their Slack message history will survive the move to Microsoft Teams. Public channel history moves; direct messages older than two years do not, under their current plan. Write a reply. Say clearly what moves, say clearly what does not, name the reason, and offer one option that would preserve the rest.'),
    ('A customer wants the migration to run during business hours because their team works nights. Running in the day is possible but will slow their file access noticeably. Write a reply. Agree to their request, describe the slowdown honestly, suggest one way to reduce the impact, and ask them to warn their staff.'),
    ('A customer says two hundred files failed to migrate and asks if their data is lost. The files failed a path-length check, are still in the source system, and will be renamed and moved this week. Write a reply. Reassure them nothing is lost, explain the cause in plain words, give a completion day, and say who is doing the work.'),
    ('A customer has not yet returned the admin credentials your team needs, and the kickoff is in three days. Without them, discovery cannot start. Write a polite chasing email. Say what you need, say why it matters to their date, give the deadline, and offer a secure way to send it.'),
    ('A customer asks for a written summary after a call in which you agreed to split their migration into three waves. Write the follow-up email. Restate the three waves and their dates, name the owner on each side, list the one open decision, and ask them to correct anything you have wrong.'),
    ('A customer complains that their users were not told about the downtime window and were locked out on Saturday morning. Your team sent the notice to their IT manager on Tuesday. Write a reply. Apologise for the disruption, state when and to whom the notice went, propose a better way to reach end users next time, and confirm the next window.'),
    ('A customer asks whether shared mailboxes will keep their permissions after moving to Microsoft 365. Permissions move, but delegates must sign in once before they take effect. Write a reply. Confirm the permissions move, explain the one-time sign-in clearly, say how they can check it worked, and give a support contact for the first week.'),
    ('A customer is worried that their Dropbox migration will breach a legal hold on some folders. Those folders are excluded from the migration and stay in place. Write a reply. Confirm the exclusion, name how many folders are affected, say what happens to them after the hold lifts, and offer to send the exclusion list.'),
    ('A customer asks you to explain a failed-items report that lists nine hundred entries. Most are system files that were never meant to move. Write a reply. Explain what the report actually counts, give the number that genuinely needs attention, say what you will do about those, and offer to walk them through the file.'),
    ('A customer wants to bring their migration forward by a week because of an office move. It is possible only if they finish user cleanup by Friday. Write a reply. Say yes in principle, state the one condition plainly, give the date you need it by, and describe what happens if it slips.'),
    ('A customer emails to say their calendar invites are showing the wrong time zone after the move. The fix is a per-user setting and takes a few minutes each. Write a reply. Explain why it happened, give the steps a user can follow themselves, offer to fix it centrally instead, and give a time frame.'),
    ('A customer has asked for daily progress reports for the rest of the migration. Daily is reasonable, but you would rather send a short fixed format each morning. Write a reply. Agree to the daily report, describe the four things it will contain, name the time it will arrive, and ask if anything else must be added.'),
    ('A customer says a manager left the company and nobody can reach their OneDrive content. The content was migrated and is accessible to their IT admin. Write a reply. Confirm the content survived, explain who can reach it now, give the steps to hand it to the new owner, and offer to do it for them.'),
    ('A customer questions why you need a list of inactive accounts before starting. Migrating dormant accounts wastes licences and time. Write a reply. Explain what you will do with the list, state the benefit in plain terms, say how they can produce it quickly, and give the date you need it.'),
    ('A customer reports that one department cannot see a shared drive that everyone else can see. The group membership did not come across for that one drive. Write a reply. Acknowledge the fault, explain what went wrong without blaming their team, give a fix time, and say how you will check the other drives.'),
    ('A customer asks for the migration to be paused for two weeks because of a company audit. Pausing is safe, but the source and destination will drift apart while it is stopped. Write a reply. Agree to pause, explain the drift in simple words, describe the catch-up pass you will run afterwards, and confirm the restart date.'),
    ('A customer wants proof that everything moved before they sign off. Your team can supply an item-count comparison per department. Write a reply. Describe what the report shows, say when they will have it, explain the small differences they should expect and why, and ask what else they need before sign-off.'),
    ('A customer says their finance team is refusing to use the new platform because a spreadsheet lost its formatting. The formatting is intact in the file, but the preview is misleading. Write a reply. Explain the difference between the preview and the file, give one step to confirm it, offer a short session with the finance team, and set a follow-up date.'),
    ('A customer asks why their migration needs a pilot group when they are confident in the plan. A pilot finds problems while they are still cheap to fix. Write a reply. Make the case in two sentences, propose a specific pilot size and duration, name what you will measure, and ask them to nominate the members.'),
    ('A customer has emailed several people at your company about the same issue and is getting different answers. Write a reply that takes ownership. Confirm you are now the single contact, state the correct position on the issue, say what happens next and by when, and give your direct number.'),
    ('A customer wants to know whether their users need to do anything on the day of the cutover. They need to sign out on Friday evening and sign in with the new address on Monday. Write a reply. Give the two user actions plainly, say what they should not do, name where the instructions can be found, and offer a one-page notice they can circulate.'),
    ('A customer says their external partners are getting bounce-backs after the domain change. The old domain still needs to accept mail for thirty days. Write a reply. Explain the cause, confirm the fix is in hand, give the time it takes to take effect, and tell them what to do with any bounce message they receive in the meantime.'),
    ('A customer asks for a discount because the migration overran by one day. The overrun was caused by their late user list, which you can evidence. Write a reply that stays professional and does not concede fault. Acknowledge their frustration, state the cause factually, explain what it cost your team, and offer a goodwill gesture that is not a refund.'),
    ('A customer wants to migrate a system your team has not worked with before. It is possible but needs a two-week assessment first. Write a reply. Be honest about the unknown, describe what the assessment covers, give the cost and duration, and say what decision it will let them make.'),
    ('A customer reports that file version history did not come across for their design team. Only the current version moves under their plan, which was documented at the start. Write a reply. Confirm what happened, point to where it was agreed, say where the old versions still exist, and offer one option if they now need them.'),
    ('A customer has asked whether you can migrate a hundred thousand items over a single weekend. The realistic figure is sixty thousand at safe speed. Write a reply. Give the honest number, explain what limits it, propose a way to still meet their deadline, and ask which content matters most if a split is needed.'),
    ('A customer emails to praise one of your engineers and asks for them on the next phase. Write a reply. Thank them warmly, say you will pass the note to the engineer and their manager, confirm whether that engineer is available for the next phase, and give the date the next phase starts.'),
    ('A customer says their helpdesk is being flooded with password questions since the cutover. The passwords did not change, but the sign-in address did. Write a reply. Explain the confusion in one sentence, give the wording their helpdesk can reuse, offer a short notice for staff, and suggest one change to reduce the calls.'),
    ('A customer is asking for a root-cause explanation of a four-hour stall during their migration. A single folder holding a hundred and ninety thousand files was being re-scanned on every pass. Write a reply for a non-technical reader. Say what stalled, explain the cause in plain words, say what was changed, and say what stops it happening again.'),
    ('A customer wants the whole company migrated on one weekend rather than in waves. It can be done, but it doubles the risk of a rollback affecting everyone at once. Write a reply. Acknowledge why they want it, set out the risk in two plain sentences, recommend an alternative, and ask for a decision by a named date.'),
    ('A customer has gone quiet for two weeks and their migration slot is about to be released. Write a reply. Note the slot politely, state what happens if you do not hear back, give the date the slot goes, and make it easy to reply with a single yes or no.'),
    ('A customer asks whether their content is encrypted while it moves. It is, both in transit and at rest, and a security summary is available. Write a reply for a reader who is not technical. Answer the question directly, avoid jargon, offer the security summary, and name who they can speak to for a deeper review.'),
    ('A customer reports that a shared calendar lost its colour categories after the move. Categories are stored per user rather than on the calendar, so they need to be reapplied once. Write a reply. Explain why this happened, give the steps to reapply them, say how long it takes, and offer to do it for the three busiest users.'),
    ('A customer wants to change the destination platform two weeks before cutover. Doing so restarts discovery and moves the date by a month. Write a reply. Confirm you can support the new platform, state the schedule impact plainly, describe the work that has to be redone, and ask them to confirm in writing before you stop the current plan.'),
    ('A customer says a competitor has quoted half your price for the same migration. Write a reply that does not discount and does not criticise the competitor. Thank them for being open, name three things your quote includes, invite them to compare scope directly, and offer a call this week.')
    ) AS v(prompt)
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM seed_state WHERE seed_key = 'l2-writing-bank-v1');

-- ---------- Customer email prompts, part 2 of 5 ----------
INSERT INTO writing_prompt (created_at, updated_at, category, prompt, level, difficulty)
SELECT now(), now(), 'Customer Email', v.prompt, 2, 'MEDIUM'
FROM (VALUES
    ('A new customer has signed and you are their first point of contact. Write the welcome email. Introduce yourself and your role, list the three things you need from them before kickoff, propose a kickoff date and time, and tell them how quickly you normally reply.'),
    ('A customer has returned the discovery questionnaire with half the questions blank. The blank ones decide how the first wave is sized. Write a reply. Thank them for what they sent, name the specific questions still needed, explain in one sentence why those matter, and give a date.'),
    ('A customer has not bought enough destination licences for the users in scope, and the shortfall is sixty seats. Write a reply. State the shortfall plainly, explain what happens to those users without licences, say how long procurement usually takes, and ask who owns that purchase.'),
    ('A customer asks why the migration slows down every afternoon. Their source platform throttles heavy accounts during peak hours. Write a reply. Explain throttling in plain words, confirm it is expected rather than a fault, say how you are working around it, and confirm the finish date still holds.'),
    ('A customer asks what a delta pass is and why one is scheduled after cutover. It moves anything that changed while the main copy was running. Write a reply. Define it in one sentence without jargon, say when it will run, say what users may notice, and confirm no action is needed from them.'),
    ('A customer reports that files inside a Teams channel are missing after the move. Channel files live in a linked site that was migrated separately and is still in progress. Write a reply. Explain the link between the channel and the site, give the current progress, name the finish time, and offer a way to reach the files in the meantime.'),
    ('A customer wants their external auditors to keep guest access after the migration. Guest access has to be re-granted in the new platform. Write a reply. Explain why the guest accounts do not carry over, list what you need from them to re-grant it, give a turnaround time, and ask for the list of auditors.'),
    ('A customer asks whether their seven-year retention policy still applies after the move. Policies are configured in the destination and must be recreated. Write a reply. Confirm the data is retained, explain that the policy itself is rebuilt, say who does that and when, and offer to review the settings with their compliance officer.'),
    ('A customer says some inbound mail is still arriving in the old system three days after cutover. Two partners are caching the old mail record. Write a reply. Explain the cause without technical detail, say what you are doing about it, give the expected clear-by date, and tell them what to do with mail that arrives in the old place.'),
    ('A customer wants multi-factor authentication switched on for everyone on cutover day. Doing it the same day would flood their helpdesk. Write a reply. Agree with the goal, explain the risk of the same-day switch, propose a staged plan with dates, and ask for their decision.'),
    ('A customer asks who is responsible for the DNS change on cutover night. Their IT team must make it; your team confirms the values. Write a reply. State the split of duties plainly, give the exact time the change is needed, say what you will send them beforehand, and name the fallback if the change is late.'),
    ('A customer has thirty old archive files sitting on a file server and wants them included. They can be included but need to be uploaded first, which adds three days. Write a reply. Confirm it is possible, describe the extra step in plain words, state the schedule impact, and ask whether they want the current date or the extra content.'),
    ('A customer reports that shared links they created before the migration will expire next month. Expiry dates carry across unchanged. Write a reply. Confirm the dates came from their own settings, explain how to extend them, say whether you can do it in bulk, and offer a report of every link expiring soon.'),
    ('A customer has hit a storage quota in the destination halfway through the migration. Their plan includes less storage than the source held. Write a reply. State the shortfall in numbers, name the two ways to resolve it, recommend one, and say what happens to the migration while they decide.'),
    ('A customer asks you to rename folders during the migration to match a new naming standard. Bulk renaming is possible but must be agreed before the copy starts. Write a reply. Confirm it can be done, explain why the timing matters, say what you need from them, and give the deadline for the naming list.'),
    ('A customer has restructured two departments mid-project and the user list you hold is now wrong. Write a reply. Explain what the stale list would cause, ask for an updated export, name the fields you need, and give the date after which a change would move the cutover.'),
    ('A customer has a change freeze over the end-of-year holidays and your cutover falls inside it. Write a reply. Acknowledge the freeze, offer two alternative windows with dates, say which you recommend and why, and ask them to confirm within the week.'),
    ('A customer security team has sent a forty-question review before allowing the migration to start. Your team can answer thirty-two from the standard pack. Write a reply. Say what you can answer immediately, name what needs a specialist, give a date for the full response, and ask whether the pack can be reviewed in parallel with discovery.'),
    ('A customer in Germany asks whether their content will leave the country during the migration. It stays in the European region throughout. Write a reply. Answer the question directly, name the region in plain terms, offer the document that evidences it, and give a contact for their data protection officer.'),
    ('A customer asks whether any part of the work is subcontracted. One reporting task is done by a partner under the same agreement. Write an honest reply. Name the task that is subcontracted, confirm the contractual protection, say who remains accountable to them, and offer the partner details if they want them.'),
    ('A customer has asked for training for eighty staff before go-live. Two one-hour sessions will cover it. Write a reply. Propose the two sessions with dates and times, say what each covers, explain how the recording will be shared, and ask them to circulate the invitation.'),
    ('A customer has completed their migration and you want feedback. Write the closing email. Thank them for a specific thing they did well, summarise what was delivered in two lines, link the short survey, and name the person who takes over routine support.'),
    ('A customer escalated to your director because a promised report did not arrive. The report was delayed by a data issue that is now fixed. Write the reply that repairs the relationship. Apologise once and plainly, explain what delayed it, confirm the report is attached, and say what you have changed so it does not recur.'),
    ('A customer asks for someone from your team to be on standby all weekend during their cutover. Standby cover is available and chargeable. Write a reply. Confirm the cover, state the hours it runs, give the cost plainly, and ask for approval before the rota is set.'),
    ('A customer reports an outage on their own network that has stalled the migration. Nothing is wrong on your side. Write a reply that is helpful rather than defensive. Confirm what you can see, say what your team has paused, ask what they need from you, and offer to restart as soon as they are ready.'),
    ('A customer wants to decommission their source platform two days after cutover. Your recommendation is thirty days, so that anything missed can still be recovered. Write a reply. Make the recommendation clearly, explain the risk in one sentence, give a date you would support, and ask them to confirm the plan.'),
    ('A customer licence for the source platform expires in eleven days and the migration needs fifteen. Write a reply. State the gap in days, explain what happens if the licence lapses mid-migration, name the two options, and ask for a decision within two working days.'),
    ('A customer asks you to confirm the whole timeline in writing after several verbal changes. Write the confirming email. List each phase with its date, mark which dates are fixed and which depend on them, name the single owner per phase, and ask for a written yes.'),
    ('A customer main contact is going on leave for three weeks in the middle of the project. Write a reply. Ask who takes decisions in their absence, list the decisions likely to arise, propose a short handover call, and confirm your own cover arrangements.'),
    ('A customer has not paid the first invoice and the second phase is due to start. Write a firm but courteous email. State the invoice number and its due date, say what starting the next phase depends on, offer to connect their finance team with yours, and give a date you need it resolved by.'),
    ('A customer asks whether they can watch the migration progress themselves. A read-only dashboard can be shared with two named people. Write a reply. Confirm the dashboard, explain what it shows and what it does not, ask for the two names, and say how they should raise anything that looks wrong.'),
    ('A customer reports that a folder they deleted before the migration has reappeared in the new system. It was restored from their own recycle bin during the copy. Write a reply. Explain how it came back, confirm it is safe to delete again, describe how to stop it repeating, and offer to check for other cases.'),
    ('A customer wants a single report showing every user who has not signed in since go-live. That report exists and takes a day to prepare. Write a reply. Confirm you can produce it, say what it will contain, give the delivery date, and suggest what they might do with the result.'),
    ('A customer asks why two users share the same email address in your migration list. One is a shared mailbox that was exported as a person. Write a reply. Explain the confusion, say how you will classify it, ask them to confirm the correct treatment, and name the deadline for that answer.'),
    ('A customer has asked whether the migration can be reversed if it goes wrong. The source is left untouched and read-only until they sign off. Write a reply. Describe the safety net in plain words, say how long it stays in place, name the decision point, and explain what a rollback would involve.'),
    ('A customer wants a summary they can send to their own board. Write the email that carries it. Give three sentences a non-technical board would understand, state the business benefit in one line, name the completion date, and offer to join the board call if useful.'),
    ('A customer asks for the migration to skip anything older than five years. That is possible and would cut the volume by a third. Write a reply. Confirm the filter, state the volume saved and the time saved, warn about the one risk of excluding old content, and ask for written confirmation.'),
    ('A customer says their staff are confused because both the old and new systems still work. The overlap is deliberate and ends on a set date. Write a reply. Explain why both are live, give the date the old one closes, say what staff should use in the meantime, and offer a notice they can send.'),
    ('A customer has asked twice for a status call at short notice and you cannot make either time. Write a reply that keeps the relationship warm. Apologise briefly for the clash, offer two firm alternatives, send the status in writing so they are not waiting, and confirm your normal availability.'),
    ('A customer reports that a hundred contacts are missing from a shared address book. Shared address books migrate last, after the mailboxes. Write a reply. Confirm the order of work, say where those contacts are now, give the date they will appear, and offer an export they can use in the meantime.')
    ) AS v(prompt)
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM seed_state WHERE seed_key = 'l2-writing-bank-v1');

-- ---------- Customer email prompts, part 3 of 5 ----------
INSERT INTO writing_prompt (created_at, updated_at, category, prompt, level, difficulty)
SELECT now(), now(), 'Customer Email', v.prompt, 2, 'MEDIUM'
FROM (VALUES
    ('A customer moving from Egnyte asks whether their locked files will migrate while users still have them open. Locked files are skipped and picked up in the delta pass. Write a reply. Explain what happens to an open file, say when it gets collected, tell them what users can do to help, and confirm nothing is lost.'),
    ('A customer moving off Citrix ShareFile asks what happens to files that were shared with clients through expiring links. Those links do not migrate and must be reissued. Write a reply. State plainly that the links stop working, give the date they stop, explain how to reissue them, and offer a list of the links in use.'),
    ('A customer asks whether their Confluence pages will keep their internal links after moving to SharePoint. Most links are rewritten automatically; links inside macros are not. Write a reply. Say what is rewritten, say what needs manual attention, give the number of affected pages, and propose who fixes them.'),
    ('A customer asks whether OneNote notebooks come across intact. They do, but each user must reopen them once for offline copies to rebuild. Write a reply. Confirm the notebooks migrate, describe the one-time step, say what a user sees if they skip it, and offer a short instruction they can circulate.'),
    ('A customer reports that filenames with accented characters look wrong in the destination. The names are correct in storage; an older desktop client is displaying them badly. Write a reply. Explain where the problem actually is, confirm the files are undamaged, give the client version that fixes it, and offer to check two examples together.'),
    ('A customer asks about files their security scanner flagged during the migration. Forty files were quarantined and not copied. Write a reply. Say how many were held and why, explain that quarantine is a protection rather than a failure, describe how to release a file they trust, and offer the full list.'),
    ('A customer has eleven files larger than the destination size limit. They cannot be copied as they are. Write a reply. State the limit in plain terms, name how many files exceed it, offer two ways to handle them, and ask which they prefer.'),
    ('A customer asks why their migration report counts more items than their own system reports. Your count includes item versions and theirs does not. Write a reply. Explain the difference in counting, give both numbers, say which one to use for sign-off, and offer a reconciled report.'),
    ('A customer asks whether shortcut files that point at network drives will still work. They will not, because the network drives are being retired. Write a reply. Explain what a shortcut depends on, confirm what breaks, describe the replacement, and give the date the network drives close.'),
    ('A customer wants to keep using a legacy application that sends mail through the old server. It needs reconfiguring before the old server closes. Write a reply. Say what has to change, name who normally does it, give the deadline, and describe what happens if it is missed.'),
    ('A customer service account is blocked by the new multi-factor policy and an overnight job has failed twice. Write a reply. Confirm the cause, describe the exemption that is normally used, say what you need to set it up, and give a time by which the job will run cleanly.'),
    ('A customer has sent a user mapping spreadsheet with fourteen rows that do not match any account. Write a reply. Explain what a mismatch does to the migration, list the kinds of error you found rather than all fourteen rows, attach the marked-up file, and ask for a corrected version by a date.'),
    ('A customer asks whether personal accounts staff created themselves will be migrated. They are out of scope and remain with the individuals. Write a reply. State the position clearly, explain the reason without sounding like a rule book, describe what a staff member should do with work content held there, and give a deadline.'),
    ('A customer asks what happens to the content of employees who have left. It migrates into an archive that only their managers can reach. Write a reply. Describe where the content goes, say who can reach it, explain how long it is kept, and ask them to confirm the retention period.'),
    ('A customer has acquired another company and wants both tenants merged. It is a tenant-to-tenant migration with a domain conflict to resolve first. Write a reply. Confirm it can be done, name the domain conflict as the first task, describe the two possible approaches, and propose a scoping call.'),
    ('A customer is divesting a business unit and needs sixty users and their content moved out. The content must be copied, then deleted from the source on a set date. Write a reply. Confirm the two-stage plan, name what evidence you will provide of the deletion, ask who signs off before deletion, and propose dates.'),
    ('A customer has two staff with the same name and one mailbox has been mapped to the wrong person. Write a reply. Apologise plainly, say what has been exposed and what has not, describe the correction and when it happens, and say what you have changed to stop a repeat.'),
    ('A customer asks whether meeting rooms and shared equipment calendars migrate. They do, but bookings more than six months ahead need re-creating. Write a reply. Confirm what moves, name the six-month boundary, say how many future bookings are affected, and offer to export them.'),
    ('A customer asks what happens to distribution lists and email aliases. Lists migrate; aliases must be recreated in the destination. Write a reply. Separate the two clearly, give the number of aliases involved, say who recreates them and when, and offer a checklist for their team.'),
    ('A customer wants their out-of-office messages preserved through the cutover. They do not migrate and must be set again. Write a reply. State the limitation honestly, say when users should set them, offer a reminder notice, and give one line of wording their staff could reuse.'),
    ('A customer asks whether mail rules and folders that users built themselves will survive. Folders migrate; client-side rules do not. Write a reply. Explain the difference in one plain sentence, say what a user should do before cutover, give a deadline, and offer instructions they can circulate.'),
    ('A customer asks whether email signatures will carry over. They are stored on each device and will need reapplying. Write a reply. Explain where signatures actually live, say what users must do, propose a company-wide signature as an alternative, and ask whether they want that.'),
    ('A customer reports that staff on mobile phones have to set up their mail again after cutover. That is expected, and there is a one-page guide. Write a reply. Confirm it is expected, say how long it takes each person, offer the guide, and suggest when they should tell staff.'),
    ('A customer asks whether their backup vendor can still reach the new platform. The vendor needs new access to be granted. Write a reply. Explain what has to change, say who grants the access, warn what an unbacked-up week would mean, and propose the date the change is made.'),
    ('A customer legal team asks how e-discovery searches will work after the move. The destination has its own search tool, and old exports remain valid. Write a reply. Answer without legal jargon, name the tool in one line, say what happens to searches already in progress, and offer a session with their legal team.'),
    ('A customer asks whether sensitivity labels on documents survive the migration. Labels migrate, but the rules behind them must be recreated first. Write a reply. Explain the order of work, say what happens if the rules are missing, give the date the rules will be ready, and ask who owns them.'),
    ('A customer wants their sharing policy tightened as part of the move, so that no file can be shared publicly. That change would break sixty existing public links. Write a reply. Confirm you can apply the policy, name the number of links affected, propose a way to review them first, and ask for a decision.'),
    ('A customer reports that some staff cannot open files that were shared with a partner domain. The partner domain is not yet on the allowed list. Write a reply. Explain the cause, say what has to be added and by whom, give the time it takes to apply, and confirm nothing was lost.'),
    ('A customer asks why their migration was paused overnight without warning. It was paused automatically when the source platform withdrew a token. Write a reply. Explain what happened in plain words, confirm the time lost, say what has been done to prevent it, and give the revised finish time.'),
    ('A customer asks for a reference from a similar migration before they commit to the next phase. Two references are available with permission. Write a reply. Offer the two references and their industries, explain how the introduction works, ask what they most want to hear about, and propose a date.'),
    ('A customer has asked whether the price would change if their volume turns out to be smaller than estimated. The commercial terms allow a review at a stated threshold. Write a reply. Explain what triggers a review, name the threshold plainly, say when the true figure is known, and offer to walk through it then.'),
    ('A customer wants a named engineer available for a call every Monday for a month. That is possible within existing hours. Write a reply. Confirm the arrangement, propose the time, name what will be covered each week, and say how to reach the engineer between calls.'),
    ('A customer asks whether a rollback would restore their old sharing links exactly. It would, because the source is untouched. Write a reply. Confirm the answer directly, explain in one sentence why it is true, say how long that remains true, and name the decision they must make before it stops being true.'),
    ('A customer has asked for the migration plan in a format their programme office can accept. They want a milestone list rather than prose. Write the covering email. Explain what is attached, name the six milestones in one line each, say which two depend on them, and ask for feedback within the week.'),
    ('A customer reports that their intranet homepage lost its images after the move. The images point at old addresses that need rewriting. Write a reply. Explain the cause in plain words, say how many pages are affected, give the time needed to fix, and ask whether the page can be taken offline while you work.'),
    ('A customer asks whether staff can keep working normally on the day of a wave migration. They can, except for the two hours around the switch. Write a reply. Give the two-hour window with times, say exactly what will not work, say what will still work, and offer a notice for staff.'),
    ('A customer has requested a change to the scope that adds four days but has not raised a change request. Write a reply. Confirm the request is understood, state the schedule and cost impact, explain that a written change request is needed, and offer to draft it for their approval.'),
    ('A customer asks what happens to files that two people edited at the same time during the copy. The later version wins and the earlier one is kept as a version. Write a reply. Explain the rule in plain words, say where the earlier version can be found, confirm nothing is discarded, and offer an example.'),
    ('A customer wants to know why you recommend moving the smallest department first. It proves the process on a group that is easy to support. Write a reply. Give the reason in two sentences, name the department you recommend, say what you will learn from it, and ask them to confirm the order.'),
    ('A customer has complained that your status reports are too technical for their sponsor. Write a reply. Accept the point without defensiveness, describe the shorter format you will send instead, confirm the detailed version still goes to their IT team, and ask which they want first.')
    ) AS v(prompt)
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM seed_state WHERE seed_key = 'l2-writing-bank-v1');

-- ---------- Customer email prompts, part 4 of 5 ----------
INSERT INTO writing_prompt (created_at, updated_at, category, prompt, level, difficulty)
SELECT now(), now(), 'Customer Email', v.prompt, 2, 'MEDIUM'
FROM (VALUES
    ('A customer asks for a call at seven in the morning their time, which is the middle of the night for your team. A colleague in another region can cover it. Write a reply. Accept the time, introduce the colleague who will attend, say what you will brief them on, and confirm you will read the notes afterwards.'),
    ('A customer has raised the same question in three separate emails today. Write one consolidated reply. Answer the question once and clearly, list the three emails you are closing with it, say who to write to for each future topic, and offer a call if anything remains unclear.'),
    ('A customer asks whether the migration will affect the performance of their existing systems. It adds load to the source platform during working hours. Write a reply. Describe the effect honestly, say when it is most noticeable, name what you can do to reduce it, and ask whether they want the schedule adjusted.'),
    ('A customer wants a written confirmation that no content was deleted during the migration. Nothing was deleted; the source is intact. Write a reply. State it plainly, describe the evidence available, offer the item-count report as support, and name who signs the confirmation.'),
    ('A customer says one of your engineers was abrupt on a call. The engineer was under pressure but the customer is entitled to better. Write a reply. Apologise without excuses, say what you will do about it internally, avoid blaming the engineer to the customer, and confirm who will lead the next call.'),
    ('A customer asks whether they can run their own test migration before signing. A limited trial of ten users is available. Write a reply. Describe the trial and its limits, say how long it takes, name what they will be able to judge from it, and propose start dates.'),
    ('A customer has asked for the raw log files from a failed batch. The logs are large and mostly noise. Write a reply. Offer the logs as they asked, explain what they will find in them, offer a summarised extract instead, and ask which they prefer.'),
    ('A customer asks why a migration that finished in two hours for one department has taken two days for another. The second department has many small files. Write a reply. Explain the difference between size and item count, give both figures, confirm both are progressing normally, and say when the second finishes.'),
    ('A customer wants to move their cutover from Saturday to Sunday with two days notice. The rota can be changed but their own staff must be told. Write a reply. Confirm the change is possible, list what has to be reconfirmed, name the deadline to lock it, and ask them to notify their staff.'),
    ('A customer asks whether the destination platform can be configured to look like their old one. Some of it can, some cannot. Write a reply. Say which parts can be matched, be honest about what cannot, suggest where training is the better answer, and offer to review the top five differences with them.'),
    ('A customer reports that a department head has refused to have their files moved. The migration cannot leave one department behind without a cost. Write a reply to the project sponsor. Set out the situation factually, explain the consequence of an exception, propose a way to reassure the department head, and ask the sponsor to decide.'),
    ('A customer asks whether the migration includes the content of their public website. It does not; the website is a separate system. Write a reply. Clarify the boundary politely, say what is in scope, name who could help with the website, and offer to arrange that conversation.'),
    ('A customer asks for the project to be extended to cover a second office of two hundred users. Write a reply. Confirm the interest, name the information you need to size it, propose a scoping call, and give an indication of lead time without quoting a price.'),
    ('A customer has told you their finance team needs a purchase order number on every invoice. Write a reply. Confirm the change, ask for the number and its expiry, say when it will appear on invoices, and name your billing contact for anything urgent.'),
    ('A customer wants weekly meetings reduced to fortnightly now that the project is stable. Write a reply. Agree with the change, propose the new pattern with dates, describe what you will send in the off weeks, and confirm how to call an extra meeting if needed.'),
    ('A customer asks whether their staff will lose access to files during the two weeks between the copy and the cutover. They will not; the source stays live. Write a reply. Reassure them clearly, explain what happens to changes made in that period, say when they must stop using the old system, and confirm the date.'),
    ('A customer has asked whether you can start on Monday even though the contract is still with their legal team. Work cannot begin without a signature. Write a reply that is firm and warm. Explain what you cannot start, name what you can do in the meantime, say what the delay costs in schedule terms, and offer to speak to their legal team.'),
    ('A customer says a folder that should hold eight hundred documents now holds seven hundred and eighty. Twenty were duplicates removed by the destination. Write a reply. Explain the difference plainly, confirm no unique document is missing, say how they can verify a sample, and offer the list of removed duplicates.'),
    ('A customer wants to know how you protect their administrator credentials. Credentials are held in a vault and access is logged. Write a reply for a security-conscious reader. Answer directly, avoid marketing language, describe how access is removed at the end, and offer the security summary.'),
    ('A customer asks whether the project can absorb a two-week pause while they replace their IT manager. It can, with a restart cost. Write a reply. Agree to the pause, describe the restart work honestly, give the cost in days rather than money, and propose a restart date.'),
    ('A customer has asked for a hard guarantee that nothing will go wrong. No honest supplier can give one. Write a reply that keeps their confidence. Say what you can commit to, describe the safeguards in place, name what would happen if something did go wrong, and offer to review the risk register with them.'),
    ('A customer asks what percentage of migrations your team completes on time. The honest answer is high but not perfect. Write a reply. Give the figure honestly, name the most common cause of delay, say what you need from them to protect their date, and offer the reference call.'),
    ('A customer wants to run the migration themselves with your team advising. That is a supported model with clear boundaries. Write a reply. Confirm the model exists, describe who does what, name the two risks they take on, and propose a call to agree the split.'),
    ('A customer has sent a long email describing five separate problems. Two are urgent. Write a reply. Acknowledge all five, name the two you are treating as urgent and why, give a time for each, and say when you will come back on the remaining three.'),
    ('A customer asks whether their content will be indexed and searchable immediately after the move. Search indexing takes up to forty-eight hours. Write a reply. Set the expectation clearly, say what works in the meantime, warn against reporting it as a fault too early, and give the time it settles.'),
    ('A customer has reported a problem that turns out to be caused by their own recent policy change. Write a reply that is helpful and not point-scoring. Describe what you found, explain the link to the policy change without accusation, recommend the correction, and offer to test it together.'),
    ('A customer asks for a formal apology letter after a failed weekend attempt. Write the email that accompanies it. Apologise in your own words first, summarise what went wrong in two lines, state the corrective action and its date, and name who owns it.'),
    ('A customer asks whether they should tell their customers about the migration. In most cases no, unless mail addresses change. Write a reply. Give the recommendation, name the one case where notice is needed, offer a draft they could adapt, and ask whether their addresses are changing.'),
    ('A customer asks how long the project team stays available after go-live. Hypercare runs for two weeks, then routine support. Write a reply. Explain what hypercare covers, give its dates, describe what changes afterwards, and give the support contact for later.'),
    ('A customer wants a copy of everything migrated held offline as insurance. That is a separate service with a cost and a storage limit. Write a reply. Confirm it can be arranged, describe what it includes, name the limits honestly, and ask whether they want a quote.'),
    ('A customer has asked you to explain why an item count dropped between two reports. The earlier report double-counted items in shared folders. Write a reply. Admit the reporting error plainly, give the corrected figure, explain what caused it, and say what has changed in the report.'),
    ('A customer asks whether staff can access the new system from home on personal devices. Their policy currently blocks it. Write a reply. State what the platform allows, note that their policy governs it, name who owns that decision, and offer to explain the options to that person.'),
    ('A customer wants to know why a small department is scheduled after a large one. The large one sets the pattern the rest follow. Write a reply. Give the reasoning in two sentences, confirm you can change the order if they prefer, name the cost of changing it, and ask for their preference.'),
    ('A customer says one user has been unable to work for a full day since the migration. Write the reply. Acknowledge the seriousness, say what has been done in the last hour, give the next update time, and offer a temporary way for that user to work today.'),
    ('A customer asks for a summary of everything still outstanding two weeks after go-live. Six items remain, four of them theirs. Write a reply that is factual rather than accusing. List the six with owners, name the two you own with dates, ask for dates on the other four, and propose a closing call.'),
    ('A customer asks whether the migration is affected by an outage their platform vendor has announced. It affects one of the two days planned. Write a reply. Confirm you have seen the announcement, say what it means for their schedule, describe your revised plan, and ask them to confirm it.'),
    ('A customer wants their logo and colours applied to the new platform before go-live. Branding is possible and takes two days. Write a reply. Confirm what can be branded, list the files you need from them, give the deadline to keep the date, and describe what happens if the files are late.'),
    ('A customer has praised the project in a public post and asks whether you would like to be named. Write a reply. Thank them sincerely, say who in your company needs to approve it, give the time that takes, and ask if a short quote from your side would help.'),
    ('A customer has asked how your team measures whether the migration succeeded. Success is measured on item counts, error rate and user reports. Write a reply. Name the three measures, say what threshold each must meet, name when they are checked, and ask whether they would add a fourth.'),
    ('A customer wants to close the project but two remediation items are still open. Write a reply. Agree the project can close, list the two open items with owners and dates, explain how they will be tracked after closure, and ask them to confirm the closure on that basis.')
    ) AS v(prompt)
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM seed_state WHERE seed_key = 'l2-writing-bank-v1');

-- ---------- Customer email prompts, part 5 of 5 ----------
INSERT INTO writing_prompt (created_at, updated_at, category, prompt, level, difficulty)
SELECT now(), now(), 'Customer Email', v.prompt, 2, 'MEDIUM'
FROM (VALUES
    ('A customer asks whether their team can keep a read-only copy of the old system for a year. It is possible if they keep a small number of licences. Write a reply. Explain what read-only access needs, give the licence count, name the yearly cost driver without quoting a figure, and ask whether to prepare a quote.'),
    ('A customer reports that a document approved last week now shows an older version as current. A user restored it by mistake. Write a reply. Explain what happened without blaming anyone by name, say how to restore the approved version, offer to do it, and suggest one control that would prevent a repeat.'),
    ('A customer asks for the migration to include a system that is being retired next month anyway. Migrating it would waste four days. Write a reply. Ask the question that matters, set out the four-day cost, recommend excluding it, and ask them to confirm the decision.'),
    ('A customer has asked whether you can work to a fixed price rather than daily rates. A fixed price is possible once discovery is complete. Write a reply. Explain why discovery has to come first, say what the fixed price would then cover, name what stays variable, and propose the date you could quote.'),
    ('A customer wants to speak to the engineer who will actually do the work before they commit. Write a reply. Agree to the introduction, name the engineer and their experience in one line, propose a short call, and say what they should ask to get the most from it.'),
    ('A customer asks what happens if their internet connection fails during the cutover. The migration runs in the cloud and does not depend on their connection. Write a reply. Answer directly, explain what their connection does affect, describe the fallback for their own staff, and confirm the plan holds.'),
    ('A customer says a report you sent contains a name of another customer. Write the reply. Apologise without minimising, ask them to delete the file, describe what has been done internally, and confirm when the corrected report will arrive.'),
    ('A customer has asked for the migration to run over four weekends rather than one, to reduce disruption. It is possible and adds a small cost. Write a reply. Confirm the approach, describe how the waves would split, name the extra cost in plain terms, and ask for a decision by a date.'),
    ('A customer wants to know why their trial migration produced errors that the real one will not. The trial ran without the exclusions the real one uses. Write a reply. Explain the difference in one sentence, name the exclusions, say what the trial did prove, and confirm the plan is unchanged.'),
    ('A customer has asked twice for a document that a colleague was supposed to send. Write the reply. Apologise for the wait, attach or name the document, explain briefly why it was delayed, and confirm who owns it from now on.'),
    ('A customer asks whether staff can be migrated in an order they choose rather than by department. They can, if the list arrives five days ahead. Write a reply. Confirm the flexibility, give the five-day rule and the reason for it, name the format you need, and ask when they can send it.'),
    ('A customer wants their migration to finish before a board meeting on the twentieth. It can, but only if approvals land this week. Write a reply. Say yes conditionally, name the approvals needed and from whom, give the date they are needed by, and state what happens if they slip.'),
    ('A customer has told you their staff do not trust the new system after a bad experience with a previous supplier. Write a reply that acknowledges it. Recognise the history without criticising the other supplier, name two things you do differently, propose a demonstration for the sceptics, and offer dates.'),
    ('A customer asks whether you will need access to individual mailboxes and what your staff can see. Access is at administrator level and content is not read. Write a reply for a privacy-conscious reader. Explain the access honestly, describe the controls on it, say how long it lasts, and offer their works council a written summary.'),
    ('A customer wants their migration reported in their own project tracker rather than yours. You can export a weekly summary they can import. Write a reply. Confirm what you can provide, describe the format, name the day it arrives, and ask who receives it.'),
    ('A customer asks why the same error appears in every daily report even though it was reported fixed. It is a stale entry in the report rather than a live error. Write a reply. Explain the difference clearly, confirm the underlying issue is closed, say what you are changing in the report, and give the date it will look right.'),
    ('A customer has requested a face-to-face visit for the go-live. Travel is possible but must be agreed and charged. Write a reply. Confirm you can attend, name what the visit would cover, be transparent that travel is chargeable, and ask whether they want it arranged.'),
    ('A customer asks for a plain-language explanation of a technical constraint to share with their staff. The constraint is that files above a size limit cannot be moved automatically. Write the email they can forward. Explain the limit without jargon, say what staff should do with a large file, name the deadline, and give a contact.'),
    ('A customer asks whether the two-day estimate for their remediation queue is working days or calendar days. It is working days. Write a reply. Answer the question directly, give the actual finish date, apologise for the ambiguity, and confirm you will state it clearly in future reports.'),
    ('A customer has escalated because a colleague of yours promised something you cannot deliver. Write the reply. Acknowledge that they were told this, explain what is actually possible, avoid criticising your colleague, and propose the closest alternative with a date.'),
    ('A customer asks for the meeting notes to be sent within an hour of every call from now on. That is achievable. Write a reply. Agree to it, describe what the notes will contain, name who sends them, and ask them to flag corrections within a day.'),
    ('A customer asks whether a delay on their side has used up the contingency in the plan. It has used most of it. Write a reply that is honest without being alarming. Say how much contingency remains, name what it protects against, describe what a further delay would cost, and ask for their help on the next deadline.'),
    ('A customer asks you to explain why an item shows as migrated but a user cannot open it. The permissions on the parent folder have not finished applying. Write a reply. Separate the copy from the permissions in plain words, give the time permissions take, offer a manual grant for the urgent user, and confirm the wider fix.'),
    ('A customer wants to add a requirement that all migrated files keep their original created date. That is supported for files but not for folders. Write a reply. Confirm what is preserved, be clear about the exception, explain why it matters little in practice, and ask whether that is acceptable.'),
    ('A customer has asked how many of their staff still have not completed the pre-migration checklist. It is sixty out of four hundred. Write a reply. Give the numbers plainly, name the departments most behind, say what happens to those users if they do not complete it, and propose a reminder date.'),
    ('A customer asks whether the migration will change any file contents. It does not; only location and permissions change. Write a reply. Answer in one clear sentence, explain what does change, offer a checksum comparison for a sample if they want assurance, and name who can arrange it.'),
    ('A customer has asked for evidence that a specific confidential folder was not accessible to your team. Access logs can show it. Write a reply. Confirm the evidence exists, describe what the log shows, give the time needed to produce it, and ask who should receive it.'),
    ('A customer wants to bring a third party in to audit the migration mid-project. That is acceptable with notice. Write a reply. Welcome the audit, name what you need in advance, describe what you will provide, and ask for the auditor contact and dates.'),
    ('A customer asks what happens to their existing support tickets when the platform changes. Open tickets stay open; the tool changes. Write a reply. Confirm nothing is dropped, explain what changes for the person raising a ticket, give the date of the change, and offer a short guide.'),
    ('A customer says the migration has been reported as complete but their own checks show a folder missing. Write a reply. Take it seriously without conceding a fault yet, say what you are checking and by when, confirm the source is still intact, and give the update time.'),
    ('A customer asks whether you can begin discovery while the security review runs in parallel. Discovery needs read access, which the review has not yet cleared. Write a reply. Explain the dependency, say what part of discovery can start without access, give the date the rest needs clearance by, and ask who chases the review.'),
    ('A customer asks whether their content will be deleted from your systems after the project. Nothing is retained beyond the reports. Write a reply. State the position plainly, describe what is retained and for how long, name the document that confirms it, and offer written confirmation on closure.'),
    ('A customer wants to know how you will handle a user who refuses to stop using the old system after cutover. The old system will be made read-only. Write a reply. Describe the control in plain words, name the date it applies, suggest how their managers should communicate it, and offer a short notice.'),
    ('A customer asks for a single point of escalation outside working hours during go-live weekend. There is a rota with a duty number. Write a reply. Give the duty arrangement, name the hours it covers, explain what qualifies as an escalation, and give the number and a backup.'),
    ('A customer says their project sponsor has changed and the new one wants a full briefing. Write the email to the new sponsor. Introduce yourself and the project in three lines, state where it stands and the next milestone, name the one decision you need from them, and propose a call.'),
    ('A customer asks whether the migration will affect an integration between their file platform and their finance system. The integration must be reconnected after the move. Write a reply. Name the effect clearly, say who reconnects it, give the expected downtime for that integration, and ask them to schedule it.'),
    ('A customer wants confirmation that a wave which finished last night is genuinely complete. Reports are ready. Write the confirmation email. State what was migrated in numbers, name the errors and how they were resolved, say what they should check today, and give a date for sign-off.'),
    ('A customer has raised a complaint about the amount of email they receive from your team. Write a reply. Accept the point, propose one daily digest instead, name what would still be sent immediately, and ask them to confirm the change.'),
    ('A customer asks whether their staff need to do anything to prepare over the next two weeks. There are three simple tasks. Write the email they can forward to staff. List the three tasks in plain words, give the deadline for each, say why they matter in one line, and name where to get help.'),
    ('A customer wants a lessons-learned session after go-live and has asked what you would raise. Two of the lessons concern them. Write the email that proposes the session. Suggest a date and duration, list four topics including the two that concern them, frame those two constructively, and ask what they would add.')
    ) AS v(prompt)
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM seed_state WHERE seed_key = 'l2-writing-bank-v1');

-- ---------- Non-email prompts, part 1 of 5: status updates and incident reports ----------
-- The second writing task is always a non-email one, so this pool is sized to match the email
-- pool: 200 tasks across the eleven categories Level 1 already uses.
INSERT INTO writing_prompt (created_at, updated_at, category, prompt, level, difficulty)
SELECT now(), now(), v.category, v.prompt, 2, 'MEDIUM'
FROM (VALUES
    ('Status Update', 'Write a status update for your manager about a migration that is two days ahead of schedule. The gain came from the customer sending their user list early. Cover what is finished, why it is ahead, what you will do with the spare time, and one risk that still stands.'),
    ('Status Update', 'Write a status update about a wave that finished with forty unresolved errors out of ninety thousand items. Cover the completion figures, what the forty errors are, when they will be cleared, and whether the next wave still starts on time.'),
    ('Status Update', 'Write a status update for a project that is blocked waiting on customer approval. Nothing has moved for four days. Cover what is complete, what is blocked and by whom, the date the delay starts costing the schedule, and what you are asking your manager to do.'),
    ('Status Update', 'Write a status update at the halfway point of a three-month migration programme. Cover the two waves already delivered with numbers, the wave in progress, the top risk for the remaining half, and the decision needed this month.'),
    ('Status Update', 'Write a status update after a week in which nothing went wrong. Avoid padding it out. Cover the work completed, the numbers that matter, what happens next week, and one thing you would like kept an eye on.'),
    ('Status Update', 'Write a status update about a migration that has consumed most of its contingency because of two customer-side delays. Cover progress in numbers, how much contingency is left, what a further delay would mean, and what you recommend.'),
    ('Status Update', 'Write a status update for a project where your own team caused a two-day slip through a configuration error. Cover the current position honestly, name the cause without blaming an individual, state the recovery plan, and give the new finish date.'),
    ('Status Update', 'Write a status update covering three customers at once for your team lead. One is on track, one is ahead, one is at risk. Cover each in two lines, name the one that needs attention, say what you need for it, and give the dates.'),
    ('Status Update', 'Write a status update for the last week before a go-live weekend. Cover what is ready, what is still outstanding and its owner, the cutover timings, and who is on duty over the weekend.'),
    ('Status Update', 'Write the first status update after a go-live. Cover how the weekend went in numbers, the issues raised by users in the first two days, what is being done about them, and when hypercare ends.'),
    ('Status Update', 'Write a status update about a pilot group of twenty-five users. Cover what the pilot proved, the two problems it found, what you changed as a result, and your recommendation on whether the full rollout should proceed.'),
    ('Status Update', 'Write a status update for a migration that has been paused for a customer audit. Cover the position when work stopped, what happens to the data while paused, the restart date, and the catch-up work restarting will need.'),
    ('Status Update', 'Write a status update where the headline number looks bad but the position is fine: sixty percent complete with two days left of a five-day window. Cover the figures, explain why the finish is still safe, name what would change that, and confirm the date.'),
    ('Status Update', 'Write a status update for a project whose scope grew by ninety users last week. Cover the original scope and the new one, the schedule effect, whether the change was approved, and what you need to keep the plan honest.'),
    ('Status Update', 'Write a weekly status update aimed at a non-technical sponsor rather than an IT reader. Cover what was achieved in business terms, what it means for their staff, what happens next, and the one thing you need from them.'),
    ('Status Update', 'Write a status update after a week in which a platform outage on the vendor side cost you a day. Cover what was lost, what was recovered, whether the finish date holds, and what you are doing to protect the remaining schedule.'),
    ('Status Update', 'Write a status update on a remediation queue of three thousand one hundred items. Cover how many are cleared, the rate per day, the projected clear-by date, and whether more hands would help.'),
    ('Status Update', 'Write a status update for a project that is finishing this week. Cover the totals delivered, the items still open at closure, who owns each of them, and what you need for sign-off.'),
    ('Status Update', 'Write a status update that has to report a missed deadline for the second time. Cover what was promised, what actually happened, what is different about the new date, and what you are doing to make sure it holds.'),
    ('Status Update', 'Write a status update for your manager while you are covering a colleague on leave. Cover where their project stands, what you have picked up, what you have left for their return, and anything that cannot wait a week.'),
    ('Incident Report', 'Write an incident report about a migration that stalled for four hours because one folder held a hundred and ninety thousand files in a flat structure. Cover what happened with times, the impact, what fixed it, and what prevents a repeat.'),
    ('Incident Report', 'Write an incident report about eleven mailboxes migrated to the wrong destination accounts because of a mapping error in a spreadsheet. Cover the facts, the exposure and how long it lasted, the correction, and the control being added.'),
    ('Incident Report', 'Write an incident report about a cutover that had to be rolled back at two in the morning. Cover the decision to roll back and who made it, the state users found on Monday, the cost in time, and the plan for the next attempt.'),
    ('Incident Report', 'Write an incident report about a service account that was locked out by a new security policy, stopping an overnight sync twice. Cover the timeline, the effect on the schedule, the fix applied, and the change that stops it recurring.'),
    ('Incident Report', 'Write an incident report about a report sent to a customer that contained another customer name. Cover exactly what was disclosed, who saw it, the actions taken within the hour, and the process change made.'),
    ('Incident Report', 'Write an incident report about forty files quarantined by a virus scanner mid-migration. Cover what was held and why, whether any threat was real, how the files were released, and what you will tell the customer.'),
    ('Incident Report', 'Write an incident report about a delta pass that overwrote newer files with older versions for one department. Cover the scope of the damage, how it was detected, how the newer versions were restored, and the safeguard added.'),
    ('Incident Report', 'Write an incident report about a downtime window that overran by three hours. Cover the planned and actual times, the reason for the overrun, who was informed and when, and what changes for the next window.'),
    ('Incident Report', 'Write an incident report about a customer whose users could not sign in for a full working morning after a domain change. Cover the timeline, the root cause, the workaround given to users, and the permanent fix.'),
    ('Incident Report', 'Write an incident report about a migration that ran against a production system when it should have run against a test copy. Cover what was affected, how it was noticed, what was reversed, and the control being introduced.'),
    ('Incident Report', 'Write an incident report about a lost weekend of migration time because an expired access token was not renewed. Cover the timeline, the warning that was missed, the immediate fix, and the monitoring being added.'),
    ('Incident Report', 'Write an incident report about a shared drive that was migrated twice, creating duplicates for two hundred users. Cover how the duplication happened, its effect on users, the clean-up, and the check that prevents a repeat.'),
    ('Incident Report', 'Write an incident report about a customer escalation that reached your director before your team knew there was a problem. Cover what the customer experienced, why it was not visible internally, what has been resolved, and the reporting change made.'),
    ('Incident Report', 'Write an incident report about a batch that reported success while silently skipping three thousand items. Cover how the gap was found, how many items were affected, the recovery, and the change to how success is measured.'),
    ('Incident Report', 'Write an incident report about permissions applied too broadly, briefly making a finance folder visible company-wide. Cover the window of exposure, who could have seen it, the correction, and the review being carried out.'),
    ('Incident Report', 'Write an incident report about an engineer working from an out-of-date runbook, producing an avoidable outage. Cover the facts without blaming the individual, the effect, the immediate correction, and the documentation change.'),
    ('Incident Report', 'Write an incident report about a migration that exhausted the destination storage quota and stopped at eighty percent. Cover what stopped and when, what the customer had to do, the restart, and the check now run before every wave.'),
    ('Incident Report', 'Write an incident report about a scheduled job that ran twice at the same time and doubled the load on a customer system. Cover the effect on their users, the timeline, what stopped it, and the scheduling change made.'),
    ('Incident Report', 'Write an incident report about a rollback plan that could not be executed because the source had already been decommissioned. Cover the situation, why the source was gone, how the position was recovered, and the rule now applied to decommissioning.'),
    ('Incident Report', 'Write an incident report about a near miss: a delete command prepared against the wrong tenant and caught during review. Cover what almost happened, how it was caught, why it was prepared incorrectly, and the safeguard now required.')
    ) AS v(category, prompt)
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM seed_state WHERE seed_key = 'l2-writing-bank-v1');

-- ---------- Non-email prompts, part 2 of 5: meeting summaries and Slack messages ----------
INSERT INTO writing_prompt (created_at, updated_at, category, prompt, level, difficulty)
SELECT now(), now(), v.category, v.prompt, 2, 'MEDIUM'
FROM (VALUES
    ('Meeting Summary', 'Write a summary of a kickoff call with a new customer. Cover who attended and their roles, the three dates agreed, the two things the customer owes you, and the date of the next meeting.'),
    ('Meeting Summary', 'Write a summary of a call where the customer asked to move their cutover by a week. Cover the request and the reason, what your team said about the impact, the decision reached, and who confirms it in writing.'),
    ('Meeting Summary', 'Write a summary of a technical call in which two engineers disagreed about the cause of a stall. Record both positions fairly, say what evidence would settle it, name who is gathering that evidence, and give the date it is due.'),
    ('Meeting Summary', 'Write a summary of a pilot review meeting. Cover what the pilot showed in numbers, the two problems raised by users, the changes agreed as a result, and the decision on whether to proceed.'),
    ('Meeting Summary', 'Write a summary of a difficult call after a failed weekend attempt. Cover what the customer said, what your team committed to, the dates given, and what is still unresolved.'),
    ('Meeting Summary', 'Write a summary of a scoping call for a possible second phase covering two hundred users. Cover what was discussed, the information still needed, who provides it, and what happens after that.'),
    ('Meeting Summary', 'Write a summary of a weekly checkpoint where nothing changed. Keep it short and useful. Cover the position, confirm the dates still hold, note the one item carried over, and give the next meeting date.'),
    ('Meeting Summary', 'Write a summary of a security review call with a customer information security team. Cover the questions they raised, which were answered on the call, which need a written response, and the date that response is due.'),
    ('Meeting Summary', 'Write a summary of a lessons-learned session after go-live. Cover the three things that went well, the two that did not, what the team will do differently, and who owns each change.'),
    ('Meeting Summary', 'Write a summary of a call where the customer sponsor changed mid-project. Cover who the new sponsor is, what was briefed, the decisions they now own, and the follow-up meeting agreed.'),
    ('Meeting Summary', 'Write a summary of a call with a department head who does not want their files moved. Record their objection accurately, the reassurance offered, what was agreed, and what happens if they still refuse.'),
    ('Meeting Summary', 'Write a summary of a planning meeting that agreed to split a migration into three waves. Cover the split and its logic, the dates per wave, the owner on each side, and the single open decision.'),
    ('Meeting Summary', 'Write a summary of a call in which the customer asked for a discount after a one-day overrun. Record what was requested, what was said about the cause, what was offered, and what the customer will confirm.'),
    ('Meeting Summary', 'Write a summary of a handover meeting between the project team and the support team. Cover what is being handed over, the open items, the support arrangements from the handover date, and who to contact for what.'),
    ('Meeting Summary', 'Write a summary of a call with a vendor whose platform outage cost you a day. Cover what the vendor said about the cause, what they committed to, what compensation was discussed, and the follow-up date.'),
    ('Meeting Summary', 'Write a summary of a training session for eighty staff. Cover what was covered, the questions asked most often, what was promised to attendees, and where the recording and guide will live.'),
    ('Meeting Summary', 'Write a summary of a call that ran out of time with three agenda items unaddressed. Cover what was decided, name the three items not reached, propose when they are picked up, and confirm nothing urgent was left.'),
    ('Meeting Summary', 'Write a summary of a call where a customer raised five problems at once. Cover all five briefly, mark the two treated as urgent, give the response times agreed, and name the owner of each.'),
    ('Meeting Summary', 'Write a summary of an internal go or no-go meeting held the day before cutover. Cover the readiness checks reviewed, the two concerns raised, the decision taken, and the conditions attached to it.'),
    ('Meeting Summary', 'Write a summary of a call with a customer legal team about retention and e-discovery. Cover what they need, what the destination platform provides, the gap between the two, and the action agreed to close it.'),
    ('Slack Message', 'Write a Slack message to your project channel reporting that the overnight wave finished cleanly. Keep it short. Give the numbers, note the one item to watch, say what happens next, and tag nobody unnecessarily.'),
    ('Slack Message', 'Write a Slack message asking a colleague in another time zone to pick up a customer issue overnight. Give the context in two lines, say what you have already tried, say what you need them to do, and say when the customer expects an update.'),
    ('Slack Message', 'Write a Slack message to your team channel warning that a customer migration is being paused for two weeks. Say why, say what stops, say what continues, and say who to ask about it.'),
    ('Slack Message', 'Write a Slack message escalating a stalled migration to your team lead during working hours. Say what is stalled and since when, what you have checked, what you think it is, and what decision you need.'),
    ('Slack Message', 'Write a Slack message to a support channel asking whether anyone has seen a specific error before. Describe the error briefly, give the context, say what you have already ruled out, and say how urgent it is.'),
    ('Slack Message', 'Write a Slack message to your channel handing over the duty phone for the weekend. Cover which customers are live, what is expected to happen, what would count as an escalation, and where the runbook is.'),
    ('Slack Message', 'Write a Slack message telling the team that a customer has complained about tone on a call. Keep it constructive rather than pointed. Say what happened, say what has been agreed with the customer, say what the team should do differently, and offer to discuss it directly.'),
    ('Slack Message', 'Write a Slack message announcing to the wider company that a large migration completed successfully. Give the customer, the scale in numbers, one thing that made it difficult, and credit to the people involved.'),
    ('Slack Message', 'Write a Slack message asking three colleagues for help clearing a remediation queue this week. Say what the work is, how long it takes, why it matters to the customer, and how they should respond if they can help.'),
    ('Slack Message', 'Write a Slack message reporting that you are stuck and need a second opinion. Say what you are trying to do, what is happening instead, what you have tried, and how long you have been on it.'),
    ('Slack Message', 'Write a Slack message to your channel about a change to the cutover time tonight. Give the old time and the new one, the reason in one line, who has been told, and what people on the rota should do.'),
    ('Slack Message', 'Write a Slack message correcting something you told the team yesterday that turned out to be wrong. State the correction plainly, say what the right position is, say what it changes, and avoid a long apology.'),
    ('Slack Message', 'Write a Slack message to a channel that has gone quiet, asking for an update on a dependency you need. Be polite but clear. Say what you need, why it blocks you, when you need it, and offer a call if that is faster.'),
    ('Slack Message', 'Write a Slack message telling the team a customer has signed off a project. Give the outcome, the numbers, the one open item that continues, and who owns it now.'),
    ('Slack Message', 'Write a Slack message to your manager while they are in a meeting, about an urgent customer decision. Keep it to four lines. Say what has happened, the decision needed, the deadline, and your recommendation.'),
    ('Slack Message', 'Write a Slack message asking the team to stop using an out-of-date runbook that caused an outage. Say which runbook, what is wrong with it, what to use instead, and when the corrected version will be published.'),
    ('Slack Message', 'Write a Slack message to a shared customer channel confirming that a fix is live. Say what was fixed, when it went live, what the customer should check, and who to contact if it looks wrong.'),
    ('Slack Message', 'Write a Slack message asking colleagues to review a plan before it goes to a customer tomorrow. Say what the plan is, what kind of feedback you want, the deadline for comments, and where the document lives.'),
    ('Slack Message', 'Write a Slack message reporting a near miss the team should know about. Describe what nearly happened, how it was caught, why it was possible, and what has changed since.'),
    ('Slack Message', 'Write a Slack message to your team saying you will be offline for two hours during a busy migration. Say when, say what is running, say who is covering, and say what to do if something breaks.')
    ) AS v(category, prompt)
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM seed_state WHERE seed_key = 'l2-writing-bank-v1');

-- ---------- Non-email prompts, part 3 of 5: internal escalations and handovers ----------
INSERT INTO writing_prompt (created_at, updated_at, category, prompt, level, difficulty)
SELECT now(), now(), v.category, v.prompt, 2, 'MEDIUM'
FROM (VALUES
    ('Internal Escalation', 'Write an internal escalation to your manager about a customer who has missed three deadlines and now blames the delay on your team. Cover the facts with dates, the effect on the schedule, what you have already tried, and the decision you need.'),
    ('Internal Escalation', 'Write an internal escalation about a migration that cannot finish because the customer will not buy sixty licences. Cover the shortfall, who you have asked and when, the schedule consequence, and what you want your manager to do.'),
    ('Internal Escalation', 'Write an internal escalation about a technical limit that your sales team promised around. Cover what was promised, what is actually possible, what the customer now expects, and the options for closing the gap.'),
    ('Internal Escalation', 'Write an internal escalation asking for a second engineer for two weeks. Cover the workload in numbers, what will slip without help, what you have already reprioritised, and the exact dates you need cover for.'),
    ('Internal Escalation', 'Write an internal escalation about a customer whose repeated after-hours calls are exhausting your team. Cover the pattern with examples, the effect on the team, what you have asked of the customer, and what you want agreed.'),
    ('Internal Escalation', 'Write an internal escalation about a vendor outage that has now cost three customers a day each. Cover the impact per customer, what the vendor has said, what you want the company to raise with them, and what you will tell the customers meanwhile.'),
    ('Internal Escalation', 'Write an internal escalation about a security policy that blocks a service account and cannot be changed by your team. Cover what is blocked, the business impact, who owns the policy, and the exemption you are requesting.'),
    ('Internal Escalation', 'Write an internal escalation about a project whose scope has grown by a third without a change request. Cover the original scope and the current one, who requested the additions, what has been absorbed already, and what you want to happen.'),
    ('Internal Escalation', 'Write an internal escalation about an unpaid invoice that is holding up phase two. Cover the invoice and its age, what has been said to the customer, the effect on the plan, and who you think should make the next call.'),
    ('Internal Escalation', 'Write an internal escalation about a runbook error that caused an outage and is still published. Cover what is wrong, what it caused, who else could hit it, and the correction you are asking for today.'),
    ('Internal Escalation', 'Write an internal escalation about a customer request that you believe is unsafe: decommissioning the source two days after cutover. Cover the request, the risk in plain terms, the advice already given, and what backing you need to hold the line.'),
    ('Internal Escalation', 'Write an internal escalation about two projects scheduled for the same weekend with one engineer between them. Cover the clash, the commitments made to each customer, the options you can see, and your recommendation.'),
    ('Internal Escalation', 'Write an internal escalation about a customer contact who is withholding information the project needs. Cover what is missing, the attempts to get it, the schedule effect, and who you would like approached instead.'),
    ('Internal Escalation', 'Write an internal escalation about a defect in the migration tool that affects every customer using one connector. Cover the symptom, how many customers are exposed, the workaround, and what you need from the product team.'),
    ('Internal Escalation', 'Write an internal escalation about an estimate you now believe was wrong by a week. Cover the original basis, what has changed your view, what it means commercially, and what you recommend telling the customer.'),
    ('Internal Escalation', 'Write an internal escalation about a customer threatening to cancel after a rollback. Cover what happened, what the customer has said, what you have committed to, and what you need approved to recover the relationship.'),
    ('Internal Escalation', 'Write an internal escalation about a compliance request you are not qualified to answer. Cover what has been asked, the deadline, why it is outside your remit, and who you believe should own it.'),
    ('Internal Escalation', 'Write an internal escalation about a colleague repeatedly missing internal deadlines that your work depends on. Keep it factual and free of personal criticism. Cover the dates missed, the effect on customers, what you have discussed directly, and what you are asking for.'),
    ('Internal Escalation', 'Write an internal escalation about a customer environment that is far worse than discovery suggested. Cover the difference between expectation and reality, the effect on the plan and cost, what you propose, and the decision date.'),
    ('Internal Escalation', 'Write an internal escalation asking to stop work on a project you believe cannot succeed as scoped. Cover the evidence, what would have to change, the cost of continuing as is, and your recommendation.'),
    ('Handover', 'Write a handover note for a colleague taking over a live migration while you are on leave for two weeks. Cover where the project stands, the three things happening while you are away, the customer contacts, and what should wait for your return.'),
    ('Handover', 'Write a handover note passing a completed project from the delivery team to support. Cover what was delivered, the open items and their owners, the support arrangements from today, and the customer expectations you have set.'),
    ('Handover', 'Write a handover note for the duty engineer covering a go-live weekend. Cover the schedule with times, the checks to run at each stage, what counts as a reason to stop, and who to call.'),
    ('Handover', 'Write a handover note for a colleague picking up a customer relationship that has been difficult. Cover the history in three lines, what the customer values, what has caused friction, and the commitments already made.'),
    ('Handover', 'Write a handover note for the engineer taking over a remediation queue of three thousand items. Cover the current position, the method being used, the two categories that need judgement, and the target clear-by date.'),
    ('Handover', 'Write a handover note when moving a project to a different regional team for time-zone reasons. Cover the state of play, the customer contacts and their working hours, the open risks, and how the two teams will stay aligned.'),
    ('Handover', 'Write a handover note for a colleague covering your projects during an unexpected absence of one week. Cover the three projects in priority order, what each needs this week, what can wait, and who else knows the detail.'),
    ('Handover', 'Write a handover note recording everything a new joiner needs to run their first migration wave. Cover the preparation steps, the run itself, the checks afterwards, and the two mistakes people usually make.'),
    ('Handover', 'Write a handover note from the pilot phase to the full rollout team. Cover what the pilot proved, the settings that were changed as a result, what is still unknown, and the recommendation on pace.'),
    ('Handover', 'Write a handover note for a customer who is taking over the remaining migration themselves. Cover what is done, what is left, the tools and access they need, and where your support stops.'),
    ('Handover', 'Write a handover note about a customer whose sponsor has just changed. Cover what the previous sponsor agreed, what the new one has been told, the decisions now pending, and the risks in the transition.'),
    ('Handover', 'Write a handover note for an engineer taking over midway through a rollback. Cover exactly what state the systems are in, what has been reversed, what has not, and what must not be touched.'),
    ('Handover', 'Write a handover note for the account team after project closure, ahead of a renewal conversation. Cover the outcome, the customer view of it, the open items, and the two things worth raising in the renewal.'),
    ('Handover', 'Write a handover note about a half-finished piece of documentation another writer will complete. Cover what is written, what is missing, the sources used, and the deadline it must meet.'),
    ('Handover', 'Write a handover note for the person taking over the customer status reports. Cover the format, the audience and their preferences, the day and time they go out, and the two things that are always asked about.'),
    ('Handover', 'Write a handover note when a project is put on hold for two months. Cover the state everything is left in, what must be kept alive, what can be shut down, and what restarting will require.'),
    ('Handover', 'Write a handover note for the engineer who will run a second wave you designed but will not be present for. Cover the design decisions and why, the settings that must not change, the checks at each stage, and what to do if throughput drops.'),
    ('Handover', 'Write a handover note passing an escalated customer to a more senior colleague. Cover the escalation history, what the customer wants, what has been offered, and your view of what would settle it.'),
    ('Handover', 'Write a handover note for a colleague taking your place on a customer call tomorrow. Cover the purpose of the call, who attends and what they care about, the two things to say, and the one thing not to commit to.'),
    ('Handover', 'Write a handover note recording the state of a migration at the end of a long shift, for the next shift to continue. Cover what completed, what is running now, what failed and was left, and the next checkpoint time.')
    ) AS v(category, prompt)
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM seed_state WHERE seed_key = 'l2-writing-bank-v1');

-- ---------- Non-email prompts, part 4 of 5: documentation and announcements ----------
INSERT INTO writing_prompt (created_at, updated_at, category, prompt, level, difficulty)
SELECT now(), now(), v.category, v.prompt, 2, 'MEDIUM'
FROM (VALUES
    ('Documentation', 'Write a short guide telling staff what to do on the day their department is migrated. Cover what they do the evening before, what they do on the morning after, what will not work during the window, and where to get help.'),
    ('Documentation', 'Write a one-page instruction for users on reconnecting mail on a mobile phone after a cutover. Cover why it is needed, the steps in order, how long it takes, and what to do if sign-in fails.'),
    ('Documentation', 'Write a checklist an engineer must complete before starting a migration wave. Cover the access checks, the capacity checks, the customer confirmations, and the point at which the wave must not start.'),
    ('Documentation', 'Write documentation explaining the difference between a full copy and a delta pass, for a non-technical audience. Cover what each does, when each runs, what users may notice, and why both are needed.'),
    ('Documentation', 'Write a runbook section for what to do when throughput drops by more than half. Cover the checks in order, what to rule out first, when to pause the migration, and who to inform.'),
    ('Documentation', 'Write documentation for the remediation process used on files that breach path-length limits. Cover how they are identified, the renaming rule, where they are placed, and how the customer is told.'),
    ('Documentation', 'Write a short internal guide on how to size a migration from a discovery report. Cover the three figures that matter, the ratio used for time, the two things that always make it slower, and the contingency to add.'),
    ('Documentation', 'Write documentation describing what happens to shared links during a migration. Cover which links survive, which do not, what the customer must do about the ones that do not, and how to produce a list of them.'),
    ('Documentation', 'Write a page for new joiners explaining the projects lifecycle from kickoff to closure. Cover the phases in order, what the customer owes at each, what the team produces, and where the templates live.'),
    ('Documentation', 'Write documentation for the go or no-go decision before a cutover. Cover the checks that must pass, who takes the decision, what a conditional go means, and how a no-go is communicated.'),
    ('Documentation', 'Write a troubleshooting note for the three most common sign-in problems after a cutover. Cover each symptom, the likely cause, the fix, and when to escalate rather than continue.'),
    ('Documentation', 'Write documentation for how customer credentials are requested, stored and removed. Cover the request, the storage, the access controls, and the removal at project end.'),
    ('Documentation', 'Write a short guide for a customer IT team on what they must do on cutover night. Cover the DNS change with timings, the confirmations they must send, the checks they should run, and who to call.'),
    ('Documentation', 'Write documentation describing what the daily migration report contains and how to read it. Cover each section, the numbers that matter, the differences that are normal, and what should trigger a question.'),
    ('Documentation', 'Write a page describing the hypercare period after go-live. Cover what it covers, how long it lasts, how to raise something during it, and what changes when it ends.'),
    ('Documentation', 'Write documentation on how to handle content belonging to employees who have left. Cover where it goes, who can reach it, how long it is kept, and who approves its deletion.'),
    ('Documentation', 'Write an internal note recording why a customer migration was designed in three waves rather than one. Cover the reasoning, the alternatives considered, the risks each carries, and the decision that was taken.'),
    ('Documentation', 'Write a guide for engineers on writing the customer-facing summary of a technical incident. Cover what to include, what to leave out, the tone to use, and who must review it before it is sent.'),
    ('Documentation', 'Write documentation explaining how item counts are calculated in your reports and why they differ from the source system count. Cover what is counted, what is not, why versions matter, and which figure to use for sign-off.'),
    ('Documentation', 'Write a short standard operating procedure for pausing and restarting a migration. Cover when a pause is appropriate, the steps to pause safely, what happens to data while paused, and the checks before restarting.'),
    ('Announcement', 'Write an announcement to all staff at a customer telling them their migration begins next month. Cover what is changing, the dates by department, what staff must do beforehand, and where questions go.'),
    ('Announcement', 'Write an announcement to staff that the old file system will become read-only on a set date. Cover the date and time, what read-only means for them, what they must move beforehand, and who to ask for help.'),
    ('Announcement', 'Write an announcement to staff about a downtime window this Saturday. Cover the exact hours, what will be unavailable, what will still work, and what to do if they must work during it.'),
    ('Announcement', 'Write an announcement telling staff that the migration finished successfully over the weekend. Cover what is now live, the two things to check on Monday morning, where the guides are, and how to report a problem.'),
    ('Announcement', 'Write an announcement that a planned cutover has been postponed by a week. Cover the new date, the reason in one honest line, what staff should ignore from the previous notice, and what stays the same.'),
    ('Announcement', 'Write an announcement to your own company that a large migration programme has completed. Cover the customer and scale, one difficulty overcome, the people who deserve credit, and what the team learned.'),
    ('Announcement', 'Write an announcement to staff about two training sessions before go-live. Cover the dates and times, what each covers, whether attendance is required, and how the recording will be shared.'),
    ('Announcement', 'Write an announcement telling staff that multi-factor authentication will be enabled next week. Cover what changes for them, what they must set up and by when, why it is happening in one line, and where to get help.'),
    ('Announcement', 'Write an announcement that the mail domain is changing and external contacts should be told. Cover the change and its date, what still works afterwards, what staff should tell their contacts, and who to ask.'),
    ('Announcement', 'Write an announcement about a temporary slowdown in file access while a migration runs during business hours. Cover when it will be noticeable, how bad it will be, how long it lasts, and what to do if work is blocked.'),
    ('Announcement', 'Write an announcement to a customer project channel that the pilot has finished and full rollout is approved. Cover the pilot outcome, what changes for the rest of the company, the rollout dates, and what is expected of departments.'),
    ('Announcement', 'Write an announcement to staff that they must complete a pre-migration checklist within two weeks. Cover the three tasks, the deadline, what happens to those who do not complete it, and where to get help.'),
    ('Announcement', 'Write an announcement that a new support process starts on Monday and the old inbox closes. Cover the new route, the closing date of the old one, what happens to tickets already open, and who to contact in an emergency.'),
    ('Announcement', 'Write an announcement to your team introducing a new colleague joining the migration desk. Cover who they are and their background in two lines, what they will work on, who they report to, and how the team can help them settle.'),
    ('Announcement', 'Write an announcement that the weekend rota is changing to cover two go-lives. Cover the change, who is on which weekend, what cover exists in the week, and how to swap if needed.'),
    ('Announcement', 'Write an announcement to staff about a short unplanned outage that has now been resolved. Cover what happened in plain words, how long it lasted, that it is fixed, and what to do if they still see a problem.'),
    ('Announcement', 'Write an announcement telling a customer department that their migration date has been brought forward by a week. Cover the new date, why it moved, what they must now do sooner, and who to speak to if it is impossible.'),
    ('Announcement', 'Write an announcement that a legacy application will stop sending mail unless it is reconfigured. Cover which application, the deadline, what happens if nothing changes, and who owns the reconfiguration.'),
    ('Announcement', 'Write an announcement to all staff that a read-only archive of the old system is available for one year. Cover how to reach it, what it contains, what cannot be done there, and the date it closes.'),
    ('Announcement', 'Write an announcement to the company thanking everyone after a demanding quarter of migrations. Cover the numbers delivered, two specific difficulties the team handled, the credit due, and what the next quarter holds.')
    ) AS v(category, prompt)
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM seed_state WHERE seed_key = 'l2-writing-bank-v1');

-- ---------- Non-email prompts, part 5 of 5: proposals, feedback and vendor escalations ----------
INSERT INTO writing_prompt (created_at, updated_at, category, prompt, level, difficulty)
SELECT now(), now(), v.category, v.prompt, 2, 'MEDIUM'
FROM (VALUES
    ('Proposal', 'Write a short proposal recommending that a customer migrate in three waves rather than one weekend. Cover the recommendation, the two reasons behind it, what it costs in elapsed time, and the decision you need.'),
    ('Proposal', 'Write a proposal for a two-week assessment of a platform your team has not migrated before. Cover what the assessment covers, what it produces, how long it takes, and what decision it enables.'),
    ('Proposal', 'Write a proposal to a customer for a read-only archive of their old system for twelve months. Cover what it gives them, what it costs them in licences, the limits, and your recommendation.'),
    ('Proposal', 'Write a proposal to your manager for adding an automated check that would have caught last months incident. Cover the incident in one line, what the check does, what it costs to build, and what it prevents.'),
    ('Proposal', 'Write a proposal to a customer recommending they exclude content older than five years. Cover the volume and time saved, the one risk, how that risk is managed, and what you need them to confirm.'),
    ('Proposal', 'Write a proposal for a pilot group of twenty-five users before a four-hundred-user rollout. Cover who should be in it, how long it runs, what you will measure, and what would make you change the plan.'),
    ('Proposal', 'Write a proposal to a customer for out-of-hours standby cover during their go-live weekend. Cover what the cover includes, the hours, how it is charged, and what happens without it.'),
    ('Proposal', 'Write a proposal to your team lead for changing the daily report format after customer complaints. Cover what is wrong with the current one, the proposed format, who it serves better, and how you would trial it.'),
    ('Proposal', 'Write a proposal to a customer for a second phase covering two hundred users in another office. Cover what you know already, what still needs scoping, an indicative timeline, and the next step.'),
    ('Proposal', 'Write a proposal recommending that a customer keep their source system live for thirty days after cutover. Cover the recommendation, the risk it removes, what it costs, and the date the decision is needed.'),
    ('Proposal', 'Write a proposal to your manager for a runbook review after two incidents traced to out-of-date instructions. Cover the evidence, the review you propose, the time it needs, and who should be involved.'),
    ('Proposal', 'Write a proposal to a customer for tightening their sharing policy as part of the migration. Cover the change, the sixty existing links it would break, how you would handle them, and what you need agreed.'),
    ('Proposal', 'Write a proposal for a fixed-price second phase now that discovery is complete. Cover what the fixed price would cover, what remains variable and why, the assumptions it rests on, and its validity period.'),
    ('Proposal', 'Write a proposal to your manager for a weekly half-hour knowledge share on the migration desk. Cover the problem it addresses, the format, the time it costs, and how you would judge whether it works.'),
    ('Proposal', 'Write a proposal to a customer for migrating a second system that is currently out of scope. Cover what it would add, the time and sequence, the effect on the current plan, and your recommendation on timing.'),
    ('Feedback', 'Write feedback to a junior engineer who handled a difficult customer call well but gave a date they could not commit to. Cover what they did well specifically, the problem with the date, what to say instead next time, and your confidence in them.'),
    ('Feedback', 'Write feedback for a colleague who has cleared a large remediation queue ahead of schedule. Be specific rather than generic. Cover what they achieved in numbers, the judgement they showed, the effect on the customer, and what you would like them to take on next.'),
    ('Feedback', 'Write feedback to a team member whose status reports are too technical for the audience. Cover the pattern with one example, why it matters to the reader, what a better report looks like, and an offer to review the next one together.'),
    ('Feedback', 'Write feedback to a colleague who missed two internal deadlines that affected your work. Keep it direct and free of personal criticism. Cover the facts, the effect, what you need from them, and what you can do to help.'),
    ('Feedback', 'Write feedback for a new joiner after their first month on the migration desk. Cover two things they have picked up quickly, one habit to develop, what to focus on next month, and how you will support it.'),
    ('Feedback', 'Write upward feedback to your manager about the amount of context switching in your week. Cover the pattern with examples, its effect on quality, what you would like changed, and what you have already tried.'),
    ('Feedback', 'Write feedback to a colleague who spotted a near miss before it became an incident. Cover what they caught, why it mattered, what their approach showed, and how you would like the team to learn from it.'),
    ('Feedback', 'Write feedback to an engineer who was abrupt with a customer on a call. Cover what happened, the effect on the customer, what you expect next time, and acknowledge the pressure they were under.'),
    ('Feedback', 'Write feedback for a peer as part of a review cycle. They are technically strong and reluctant to ask for help. Cover their strengths with evidence, the cost of not asking, one specific suggestion, and what you value about working with them.'),
    ('Feedback', 'Write feedback to a colleague whose documentation you had to rewrite before it went to a customer. Cover what was good in the draft, what needed changing and why, the standard it needs to meet, and an offer to work through the next one together.'),
    ('Feedback', 'Write feedback to a team member who took ownership of an incident that was not theirs. Cover what they did, the judgement it showed, its effect on the outcome, and what recognition you are giving it.'),
    ('Feedback', 'Write feedback to a colleague who consistently answers customer questions faster than promised. Cover the pattern, what it means for the customer relationship, one caution about setting expectations too high, and your thanks.'),
    ('Feedback', 'Write feedback requested by a customer on how your two teams worked together. Cover what worked, the two things that cost time, what you would suggest for the next project, and what you valued.'),
    ('Feedback', 'Write feedback to a colleague who disagreed with you publicly in a customer meeting. Cover what happened, why the setting mattered, how you would like disagreements handled, and confirm you want the disagreements themselves.'),
    ('Feedback', 'Write feedback to a junior colleague who is ready for more responsibility. Cover the evidence, the specific responsibility you propose, what support comes with it, and what you will be watching for.'),
    ('Vendor Escalation', 'Write an escalation to a platform vendor whose outage cost three of your customers a day each. Cover the outage and its effect, what you were told and when, what you now need from them, and the date you need it by.'),
    ('Vendor Escalation', 'Write an escalation to a vendor about an API limit that is halving your migration throughput. Cover the technical effect in numbers, the customer impact, what you have already tried, and the increase you are requesting.'),
    ('Vendor Escalation', 'Write an escalation to a vendor whose support has not responded to a ticket in four working days. Cover the ticket and its age, the business impact, the response you expected, and what you will do if there is no reply.'),
    ('Vendor Escalation', 'Write an escalation to a vendor about a defect in their connector that silently skips items. Cover the symptom and how you found it, the customers affected, the workaround you are using, and what you need fixed and by when.'),
    ('Vendor Escalation', 'Write an escalation to a vendor who changed an interface without notice, breaking your overnight jobs. Cover what broke, when, what notice you would have expected, and what you want agreed about future changes.'),
    ('Vendor Escalation', 'Write an escalation to a vendor about invoices that do not match the agreed rates. Cover the discrepancy with figures, the term it conflicts with, what you have already asked, and the correction you require.'),
    ('Vendor Escalation', 'Write an escalation to a vendor whose maintenance window clashes with your customer go-live. Cover the clash with dates and times, why your window cannot move, what you are asking them to do, and your fallback.'),
    ('Vendor Escalation', 'Write an escalation to a subcontractor who delivered a report late and incomplete. Cover what was agreed, what arrived, the effect on your customer, and what you require before the next deliverable.'),
    ('Vendor Escalation', 'Write an escalation to a vendor about a security question their team has left unanswered for two weeks, blocking a customer review. Cover the question, the blockage it causes, the deadline, and who you will involve if it is missed.'),
    ('Vendor Escalation', 'Write an escalation to a vendor after a repeated fault that has now occurred three times. Cover the three occurrences with dates, what was promised after each, why the pattern is unacceptable, and what you want done differently.')
    ) AS v(category, prompt)
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM seed_state WHERE seed_key = 'l2-writing-bank-v1');

